answer:
## What the law said on 1 October 2025

The applicable document is the **Residential Tenancies Act 1997, Authorised Version No. 110**, incorporating amendments as at **6 August 2025** [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).

- The property had to be advertised or offered at a **fixed rent** [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- The rental provider or agent could not **solicit or invite** anyone to offer more than the advertised rent. Breach carried a maximum penalty of **60 penalty units for a natural person or 300 penalty units for a body corporate** [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- Advertising could not make a **false or misleading representation about the rent** [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- However, on 1 October 2025, the Act did **not yet prohibit accepting an unsolicited higher offer**. That prohibition appears only in the later **Authorised Version No. 111 dated 25 November 2025**, as new section 30F(4), so it does not govern your situation [[19]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf).
- The Act does not say that applying at the advertised price guarantees you the property, and the retrieved documents do not expressly prohibit withdrawing one advertisement and later advertising at a different fixed rent [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- The false email saying that **you withdrew** is concerning, but the retrieved Act does not expressly create a remedy for that statement alone. It may support an argument that the original rent advertising or application process was misleading, depending on the surrounding evidence [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).

## What you can do or ask

You have a basis to challenge or report the conduct **if there is evidence that the provider or agent invited higher bids**, or that the original advertised rent was false or misleading [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf). Merely hearing that another applicant voluntarily offered more would not establish a breach of the law then in force [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).

Ask the agent in writing to:

1. correct the record that you did not withdraw;
2. confirm who marked your application as withdrawn and when;
3. explain why the property was relisted at a higher rent; and
4. confirm whether any applicant was asked, encouraged or invited to offer above the advertised amount.

The retrieved documents do not clearly identify a specific VCAT application or remedy for an unsuccessful applicant alleging a breach of sections 30F or 30G, so I cannot say from these documents alone that VCAT could order the property to be rented to you.

## Evidence to save

Keep original, dated copies of:

- the first advertisement showing the property, advertised rent and date;
- your completed application and submission confirmation;
- all emails, texts and portal notifications, especially the false withdrawal email;
- your written response stating that you did not withdraw;
- the later advertisement showing the higher rent and its publication date;
- any message or application field inviting applicants to nominate a higher rent;
- notes of phone calls, including date, time, person and exact words used;
- any direct statement from the other applicant about what they offered and whether the agent invited it; and
- screenshots with the full URL, date and time visible where possible.

These materials address the key legal questions: what fixed amount was advertised, whether higher offers were solicited or invited, and whether the rent advertising was false or misleading [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
